course: world
lesson: 112
title: Leap years and 29 February birthdays
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Earth takes about 365 days and 6 hours to go once round the Sun, but a calendar can only count whole days. So every year the calendar falls about 6 hours behind, and after 4 years that makes 4 × 6 = 24 hours, a whole day. To catch up, February gets an extra day, 29 February, in a leap year of 366 days.
explain: Most years that divide exactly by 4 are leap years: 2024 ÷ 4 = 506 and 2028 ÷ 4 = 507, so both are; 2026 ÷ 4 = 506.5, so it is not.
explain: The extra 6 hours is a little too much: the real year is about 365 days, 5 hours and 49 minutes. So there is a rule inside the rule: years ending in 00 are leap years only if they also divide by 400. That is why 1900 was not a leap year, but 2000 was, and 2100 will not be.
explain: People born on 29 February, called leaplings, have their real birthday date only once every 4 years, but they still grow one year older every year. Olivia, born on 29 February 2016, is not "only 2": in 2026 she turns 10. In ordinary years leaplings choose 28 February or 1 March for the party.
explain: Without leap days the calendar would slowly slide away from the seasons: in 100 years it would be out by about 24 days, and after a few hundred years summer months would land in spring.
fact: The Earth takes about 365 days and 6 hours to go once round the Sun.
fact: A leap year has 366 days instead of 365, with an extra day, 29 February.
fact: 6 hours a year for 4 years makes 24 hours, one whole day, which is why there is a leap year about every 4 years.
fact: Years that divide exactly by 4 are usually leap years: 2024, 2028, 2032 and 2036 are; 2026 is not.
fact: A year ending in 00 is a leap year only if it divides exactly by 400, so 1900 and 2100 are not leap years but 2000 was.
fact: The real year is about 365 days, 5 hours and 49 minutes, a little less than 365 and a quarter days.
fact: A person born on 29 February is called a leapling and gets one year older every year, like everyone else.
fact: In years without 29 February, leaplings usually celebrate on 28 February or 1 March; in the UK a leapling is usually counted as turning a new age on 1 March.
fact: A leapling born in 2016 has real 29 February birthdays in 2020, 2024, 2028 and 2032, and turns 16 on the fourth of them.
fact: People paid the same amount every month get no extra pay for 29 February, so for them it is an unpaid extra working day.
fact: An old custom, said to come from Ireland, says a woman may propose marriage on 29 February; today anyone can propose on any day.
fact: Only one school year in every 4 contains a 29 February: the school year from September 2027 to July 2028 does, and the one from September 2026 to July 2027 does not.
fact: Without leap days the calendar would drift by about 24 days in 100 years (about 25 if you use 6 hours a year), slowly sliding away from the seasons.
fact: Words people use: "leapling" means someone born on 29 February, "craic" means fun (Ireland), "eejit" means a silly person (Ireland) and "highkey" means very openly, the opposite of "lowkey".
exercise: How long does the Earth take to go once round the Sun? | About 365 days and 6 hours | Exactly 365 days | About 366 days and 6 hours | Exactly 360 days
exercise: How many days does a leap year have? | 366 | 365 | 367 | 372
exercise: Why does a leap year come about every 4 years? | 4 lots of 6 extra hours make a whole day | The Moon goes round 4 times faster | Every fourth year the Sun moves a bit further away | Kings once liked the number 4
exercise: Olivia was born on 29 February 2016. How old does she turn in 2026? | 10 | 2 | 3 | 40
exercise: In a year without 29 February, when might Olivia celebrate? | 28 February or 1 March | 29 March or the first of April | 31 February | Not at all that year
exercise: Is 2028 a leap year? | Yes, 2028 ÷ 4 = 507 exactly | No, 2028 cannot be divided by 4 | No, only years ending in 0 are leap years | Yes, but only in some countries
exercise: Why was 1900 NOT a leap year? | It ends in 00 and does not divide by 400 | It does not divide exactly by 4 | Leap years only started a little after 1900 | It was a war year, so it was skipped
exercise: Why was 2000 a leap year? | It ends in 00 and divides by 400 | All years ending in 0 are leap years | It was the start of a new millennium | It was the first leap year ever
exercise: What is the real length of a year, more exactly? | About 365 days, 5 hours and 49 minutes | Exactly 365 days and 6 hours, to the second | About 365 days and 12 hours | Exactly 366 days
exercise: Ewan's mum is paid the same every month. Why does she grumble in a leap year? | She works an extra day in February for no extra pay | February is shorter, so she loses pay | She is paid twice in February | Leap years have no paydays
exercise: According to an old Irish custom, what could a woman do on 29 February? | Propose marriage | Stay home from work | Vote for the first time | Eat no food all day
exercise: A leapling born in 2016 has a 4th real 29 February birthday. How old is she then? | 16 | 4 | 8 | 12
exercise: How much longer is a leap year than a normal year? | 1 day | 1 week | 1 month | 6 hours
exercise: Which school year contains 29 February 2028? | September 2027 to July 2028 | September 2028 to July 2029 | September 2026 to July 2027 | Every school year
exercise: About how far out would the calendar be after 100 years without leap days? | About 24 or 25 days | About 6 hours, no more | About 1 day | About 100 days
exercise: What would happen to the seasons if we never had leap years? | The calendar would slowly slide away from them | The seasons would happen twice a year | Summer would get longer and hotter | Nothing; the seasons always follow the calendar
test: Olivia's mate: "Born on 29 Feb 2016? So you're only 2, lol." What's the truth? | She's 10; she gets a year older every year | She's 2, because she's had two real birthdays | She's 5, because birthdays come every 2 years | She's 40, because each birthday counts 4 years
test: Ravi checks: "2028 ÷ 4 = 507, leap year, yes!" Then 2100 ÷ 4 = 525. "Leap year too!" What did he forget? | Years ending in 00 must also divide by 400 | 2100 is too far away to count | Leap years only happen in even centuries | Nothing; 2100 is a leap year
test: Ravi: "1900 wasn't a leap year but 2000 was? That's just mean." What's the real reason? | The rule keeps the calendar matched to the Sun | Someone forgot about 1900 by mistake | 2000 was special because of fireworks | The rule was invented in 1950
test: Grace: "A leap year has an extra week." Her mate: "Nah, an extra month." Who's right? | Neither; it has one extra day | Grace; it has an extra week | The mate; it has an extra month | Both; it adds a week and a month
test: Ciara's gran: "Back in my day 29 Feb was the only day a girl could propose, what craic!" What is true today? | It's an old custom; anyone can propose any day | It is still the law in Ireland | Proposals made on any other day still do not count | Only men may propose on 29 February
test: Tanvir: "A leapling born in 2016, how old on their 4th real birthday?" His mate says, "4, ya eejit." Who's right? | Tanvir's sum: they turn 16 in 2032 | The mate: they turn 4, one per real birthday | Nobody: they turn 8 | Nobody: they turn 20
test: Joe: "Is there a 29 February in every school year?" What's the answer? | No, only in one school year out of every 4 | Yes, every school year has one | No, school years never contain a 29 February | Yes, but only in Scotland
test: Jade: "No leap years for 100 years and we'd be about 25 days out." Is that roughly right? | Yes, about 24 to 25 days | No, only about 6 hours out | No, about 100 days out | No, nothing would change at all
test: What would happen if we added a leap day every single year? | The calendar would run ahead of the Sun very quickly | The calendar would match the Sun perfectly | Every year would have 365 days | February would vanish completely
test: Ewan's mum, highkey annoyed: "Brilliant, a whole extra day in February for nothing." Why does she say it? | Her pay is the same each month, but she works one more day | February has fewer days in a leap year | She must pay a tax on 29 February | Leap days are always bank holidays
test: A cartoon says leap years exist "so February isn't lonely". Put it right. | They keep the calendar in step with the Earth's trip round the Sun | They exist because February is the shortest month | They exist to give people a free holiday | They exist because the Moon is late every year
exam: Determine the number of days comprised in a leap year. | Three hundred and sixty-six | Three hundred and sixty-five | Three hundred and sixty-seven | Three hundred and seventy-two
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the year 1900 was not designated a leap year. | It concludes in 00 and is not exactly divisible by 400 | It is not exactly divisible by 4 | Leap years were not instituted until after 1900 | It was omitted owing to an administrative error
exam: Whereas a person was born upon 29 February 2016, ascertain the age attained upon the fourth occurrence thereafter of that date. | Sixteen years | Four years | Eight years | Twelve full years
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the duration of the Earth's revolution around the Sun. | Approximately 365 days, 5 hours and 49 minutes | Precisely 365 days | Precisely 366 days | Approximately 360 days and 6 hours
